00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:52.960
market is showing modest resilience today's price action perfectly illustrates why disciplined

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:58.920
systematic rebalancing matters more than ever in an environment that can flip from complacent to volatile

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:05.360
in a single headline. So let's talk about exactly what systematic portfolio rebalancing looks like in

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.740
practice because this is one of the highest leverage habits any individual investor can adopt. At its

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:16.200
core rebalancing means periodically resetting your asset allocation back to your original target weights.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:22.680
If you decided on a 6040 stocks to bonds mix or a 70-2010 split across Canadian US

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:29.240
and international equities rebalancing forces you to sell whatever has run up the most and buy whatever

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:36.020
has lagged. This is the purest expression of the classic sell high by low discipline and it also acts

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:42.180
as a powerful risk control mechanism by preventing dangerous allocation drift. Let me give you a real

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:47.200
world example that many Reddit investors lived through. Imagine an investor who let their position

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:54.520
in VXF, the Vanguard extended market ETF that gives exposure to small and mid cap US stocks,

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:59.860
ballooned to 20% of their portfolio because they were convinced lower interest rates were coming and

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:06.900
small caps would finally have their day, then 2022 hit. Small caps got crushed while mega cap tech held

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:12.000
up relatively better and that investor suddenly found themselves far more exposed to the underperforming

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.920
part of the market than they ever intended. In today's environment where mega cap technology

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:22.420
continues to dominate indices while small caps lag that kind of drift can quietly destroy

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:29.220
portfolio performance and sleep at night factor for years. Implementing a rebalancing policy inside

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:36.860
a TFSA or RRSP is actually quite straightforward. You can simply set calendar reminders for quarterly

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:42.920
or annual rebalancing dates. Alternatively, many investors prefer a percentage threshold rule.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:49.080
Any time an asset class drifts more than 5% away from its target weight, you rebalance it back.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:55.380
On modern Canadian platforms like Questrade or WealthSimple, you can pull up your current allocation

00:05:55.380 --> 00:06:01.500
percentages instantly and execute commission-free ETF trades inside tax sheltered accounts.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:07.720
Historical back tests of traditional 6040 portfolios have shown that systematic rebalancing after

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:13.180
periods of strong growth versus value divergence has typically reduced maximum drawdowns by roughly

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:18.620
1-3 percentage points per year. That might not sound like much, but over decades it compounds into

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:24.460
meaningful wealth preservation. Of course, there are risks. In taxable accounts outside registered

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:30.140
plans, you have to be extremely careful about triggering the superficial loss rule or creating

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:36.080
unnecessary capital gains. Even inside registered accounts, there's the behavioral risk of selling

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:41.360
strong performing secular winners too early during a multi-year bull market. The art is finding the

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.300
balance between mechanical discipline and allowing genuine long-term trends to run.

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:52.820
Speaking of hidden costs that quietly erode retirement portfolios, let's spend some time on something

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:58.360
most retail investors still dramatically underestimate. Bid-ask spreads and slippage.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:04.300
Imagine you placed a market order for 500 shares of a thinly traded travel stock immediately after

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:10.940
last week's disappointing earnings release. Your order filled at $18.45 per share yet the official

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:18.980
last trade printed at $18.32. That's six-cent difference. Might look small until you realize the bid-ask

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:24.780
spread had blown out from just three cents to 28 cents in the first 15 minutes after the news hit

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:31.700
the tape. On that relatively small trade, you gave up an extra $65 in invisible execution cost.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:38.120
Every stock or ETF has a highest bid that buyers are willing to pay and a lowest ask that sellers are

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:43.760
willing to accept. The difference between those two prices is the spread and it represents a real transaction

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:49.820
cost even when your brokerage advertises commission-free trading. On highly liquid large cap names,

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:56.660
the spread might be only one or two cents representing 0.01% to 0.03% of the share price.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:01.940
But on lower volume names, especially right after earnings or during geopolitical headlines,

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:08.380
spreads can easily expand to 0.5% or even 1.5% effective cost on market orders.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:12.840
Professional traders always check the level two quote and time and sales window before

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:17.820
hitting the buyer cell button on anything outside the top 100 most liquid securities.

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:22.260
The most expensive times of day are usually the first and last 30 minutes of trading

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:27.640
when liquidity is thinner and news flow is highest. The single biggest mistake I see is using

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:33.740
market orders during earnings reactions or fast-moving geopolitical events. Instead, disciplined investors

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:38.580
place limit orders somewhere between the current bid and ask and remain willing to walk away

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:44.480
entirely if they cannot get filled within their maximum acceptable slippage. This same disciplined

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:49.620
approach to risk and execution is exactly why we are sitting on the sidelines in today's practice

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:54.420
investment of the day. Practice investment of the day, trade type, no trade today.

